-- A bodiless `DECLARE T` is an opaque nominal type, like Haskell's `data T`
-- with no constructors: no fields, no alternatives, no synonym body. It names
-- a type and says nothing about its values. This is the ruled spelling for the
-- type role of `ASSUME T IS A TYPE` (IMPLICIT-PROPS-DESIGN.md §11.1, RULED
-- 2026-09-05); the two produce the same entity in the type checker.
--
-- Every parser position is exercised below: a bodiless DECLARE followed by
-- another DECLARE, by a GIVEN, by a DECIDE, by a section header, and at the
-- very end of input; nullary and parameterised; with and without an explicit
-- GIVEN on the head; and one carrying an AKA alias.

§ `Opaque types`

DECLARE Person
DECLARE Premises

-- A function over an opaque type type-checks: the type is nominal, so `Person`
-- unifies only with `Person`.
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a person` p IS TRUE

-- An alias works on the bodiless form too.
DECLARE Applicant AKA Candidate

GIVEN a IS AN Applicant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is an applicant` a IS TRUE

GIVEN c IS A Candidate
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a candidate` c IS TRUE

§ `Parameterised opaque types`

-- The head's argument is a type variable. An explicit `GIVEN x IS A TYPE` is
-- optional, exactly as it is for a record head.
DECLARE Bag x

GIVEN x IS A TYPE
DECLARE Pair x

GIVEN bag IS A Bag OF NUMBER
      pr  IS A Pair OF STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `holds both` bag pr IS TRUE

§ `Mixing with records and enums`

DECLARE Licence IS ONE OF granted, refused

DECLARE Application
  HAS applicant IS A Person
      premises  IS A Premises
      outcome   IS A Licence

-- A rule that never inspects the opaque fields evaluates normally, even though
-- no value of `Person` or `Premises` can be written down in this module.
GIVEN app IS AN Application
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `was granted` app IF app's outcome EQUALS granted

§ `Under a section, and last in the file`

DECLARE Officer

GIVEN o IS AN Officer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is on duty` o IS TRUE

#CHECK `is a person`
#CHECK `is an applicant`
#CHECK `is a candidate`
#CHECK `holds both`
#CHECK `was granted`
#CHECK `is on duty`

#EVAL `was granted` (Application WITH applicant IS `some person`, premises IS `some premises`, outcome IS granted)
#EVAL `was granted` (Application WITH applicant IS `some person`, premises IS `some premises`, outcome IS refused)

-- Values of an opaque type come from outside the module: an ASSUME term here,
-- a JSON input at a service boundary in production. They cannot be evaluated
-- in-module, which is why the two directives above never look at them.
ASSUME `some person` IS A Person
ASSUME `some premises` IS A Premises

-- The bodiless form at the very end of input.
DECLARE Sentinel
